    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27764v1</id>
    <title>Enhancing Attention with Higher-Order Attention</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T15:51:54Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27764v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27764v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Current decoder large language models rely on a combination of attention, feed forward and residual layers. The attention used is autoregressive (AR) though this is not strictly correct, as we note. That motivates the introduction of other attention methods that, while also not strictly AR, do enhance the AR character. One is based on a decomposition of mutual information (MI) into a set of Conditional MIs (CMIs) that correlate key tokens with pairs of query tokens. A second, higher-order attention, introduces correlations between contiguous key and query tokens. A third introduces correlations between a set of key and query tokens and uses a Generalized Maximum Entropy Principle to obtain an attention expression spanning a sequence of tokens. All these methods may enhance the information content of the autoregressive probabilities that are used in inference/generation. Applications to contrastive decoding and a systematic analysis of autoregressive probabilities are presented.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-20T15:51:54Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>R. I. Cukier</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27762v1</id>
    <title>From Novel Combinations to Informative Absence: Structural permission, communicative opportunity, and form choice in language learning</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T15:48:08Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27762v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27762v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Language learners must infer which novel expressions are grammatically licensed and when continued absence is evidence against them. Our generative theory unites these capacities via counterfactual expression opportunities, decomposing utterance generation into structural permission, communicative opportunity, and form choice. First, shared predictive continuations recover structural permission for unseen word-construction pairings across 30 artificial grammars (mean AUC 0.945 after 120 sentences). Second, under hierarchical uncertainty, we derive a joint evidence law linking omissions to context diversity and within-context repetition, and an exact finite-sample context-refinement inequality. Thus, observing related expressions calibrates expected use to sharpen negative evidence, and higher expected use can drive steeper posterior declines for stronger-permission candidates (reversing initial order after six omissions in a fixed-rate example). Third, we prove that while raw counts confound semantic availability and form selection, scene-level opportunity observations identify both. Empirically, reanalyzing adult and child artificial-language experiments shows meaning-conditioned restriction; judgments of restricted combinations improve held-out production predictions across 399 participants (+0.036 nats/production). Naturalistic child-directed and child speech (11 families, six corpora) shows pervasive source-level usage clustering beyond lexical, number, and dependency-role controls, grounding hierarchical rate uncertainty. Finally, modeling causative judgments across five languages shows that structural compatibility and calibrated usage probabilities draw on distinct distributional, semantic, and discriminative cues. Together, these findings show how the organization and calibration of communicative opportunities govern the evidential value of linguistic absence.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="q-bio.NC" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-20T15:48:08Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yanzhe Zhao</name>
    </author>
    <arxiv:doi>10.5281/zenodo.24400851</arxiv:doi>
