  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27750v1</id>
    <title>RoSci: Exploring Robustness in Science VLMs</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T15:41:48Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27750v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27750v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Recent advancements in Vision-Language Models (VLMs) enable significant progress in scientific tasks. However, these models are generally assumed to be presented with complete and accurate information, overlooking the challenges of erroneous/missing information in wild scenarios. To address this gap, we introduce the concept of Scientific Model Robustness, which defines a model's ability to detect anomalies, assess solvability, and perform self-calibration when faced with Information Error (inconsistencies) or Information Loss (insufficiency). Specifically, we present RoSci-Bench, a diagnostic benchmark of 1,220 samples using a seed-variant strategy across six degradation types. To address VLM fragility, we further propose RoSci-Agent, a framework that employs a Parse-Verify-Decide pipeline to vet premises before reasoning. The evaluated VLMs sometimes answer questions whose premises are invalid or incomplete. RoSci-Agent achieves a higher Overall Robustness Score than the evaluated standalone VLMs. RoSci-Bench gives the multimodal reasoning community a common test of when models answer, request clarification, or reject a scientific question.</summary>
