    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27746v1</id>
    <title>Shared Understanding in Human-AI Teams: Coordination Continuity in Evolving Work</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T15:39:11Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27746v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27746v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>People are increasingly working with AI agents as part of extended, asynchronous workflows. In these settings, shared access to information does not guarantee a shared basis for action: goals, plans, and roles may persist while their current significance changes. We introduce coordination continuity, the extent to which collaborators sustain a workable basis for interdependent action as work progresses. Drawing on research on collaborative work, along with two qualitative team studies and a reflexive case from our own repository-based environment, we present a framework of three elements: durable representations of collaborative work; relative coordination properties that support interpretation of representations in the moment; and interaction mechanisms through which the status of representations can be understood and altered. We use a build-act-drift-rebuild heuristic and the notion of consequential moments to characterise when rebuilding may be needed. We draw eight design implications for collaborative AI infrastructure.</summary>
    <category term="cs.HC" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
