    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27738v1</id>
    <title>Average-Depth Variable Execution via Request-Local Token Routing</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T15:35:08Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27738v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27738v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Layer skipping can reduce large language model inference costs, but a request budget does not specify how to allocate layer execution across generated tokens. We introduce \method{}, which allows tokens to execute different numbers of layers while matching each request's mean executed depth to a continuous budget. At each routed layer, a router reads the current token state and its request's budget and decides whether to execute the Transformer block. Tokens that skip the block pass through a lightweight residual adapter and write no key/value (K/V) entry at that layer. A single checkpoint serves requests with different budgets in the same batch. Across three LLaMA backbones, six quality metrics, and 13 budgets, AVERT is best or tied for best in 206 of 234 comparisons. Across single- and mixed-budget batches, the mean absolute per-request budget error is at most 1.15 percentage points. When grouping requests by budget leaves batches underfilled, mixing budgets reduces decode time per output token (TPOT) by 4.3--21.2%.</summary>
