    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27734v1</id>
    <title>AutoOntologyBench: A Task-Grounded Benchmark for Agentic Knowledge Engineering</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T15:29:27Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27734v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27734v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Foundation-model agents are increasingly extending beyond software engineering (SWE) into autonomous scientific research, where acquiring and integrating domain knowledge remains a critical challenge. Recent industrial advances have increasingly adopted knowledge graphs and ontology-based semantic layers to organize heterogeneous knowledge into reusable representations. We ask: Can autonomous agents construct ontologies that help them solve real tasks, and do they offer measurable benefits over alternative knowledge representations? Existing benchmarks evaluate ontology correctness or agent task success in isolation, leaving the value of autonomously acquired knowledge unresolved. We therefore introduce AutoOntologyBench, a task-grounded benchmark comprising 19,947 audited tasks grounded in 18 real-world knowledge sources across 7 domains, with source-native verification. Spanning Application, Repair, Organization, and Construction, AutoOntologyBench compares nine frontier-model agents under matched knowledge representations. Layered on retrieval, agent-built ontologies add 6.1 points of task success, yet trail an expert-derived reference by 13.0, identifying autonomous knowledge acquisition as the bottleneck to scalable scientific research. Code and resources are available at https://github.com/VinyehShaw/AutoOntologyBench.</summary>
