    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27652v1</id>
    <title>Auditing Post-Training Unlearning in Diffusion Language Models using Sparse Autoencoders</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T14:17:08Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27652v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27652v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Machine unlearning is an approach to privacy protection: it aims to remove the influence of designated data while preserving the model's behavior on information that should be retained. While a growing body of work investigates this objective in autoregressive large language models, machine unlearning remains underexplored in diffusion language models (DLMs). DLMs generate text through iterative denoising, exposing partially masked states whose internal representations change throughout the generation process. This raises a DLM-specific question: can selective forgetting improve by identifying forget-data-associated features and adapting their suppression across denoising stages? We conduct a controlled investigation of this question in LLaDA-8B-Instruct. To evaluate the unlearning of knowledge plausibly encoded during pre-training, we use WMDP as our forget-domain benchmark. Using top-k sparse autoencoders and denoising-state replay, we compare static and stage-adaptive feature suppression. We find substantial shifts in forget-data-associated features across denoising stages, but tracking these dynamics alone does not reliably improve the forget-retain trade-off at the evaluated operating points. Together, our study provides an initial empirical foundation for developing and evaluating machine unlearning as a post-training privacy mechanism in DLMs.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-20T14:17:08Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>14 pages, 1 figure. Accepted at the NeurIPS 2026 Workshop on Inference-Time AI Privacy (InfPriv 2026)</arxiv:comment>
