    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27576v1</id>
    <title>Unlocking Heterogeneous Accelerator Flexibility in AI Data Centers: Sizing and Workload Allocation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T13:12:31Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27576v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27576v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>AI data centers are an emerging electricity demand, but limited local grid capacity could delay their connection and expansion. While existing operational strategies consider spatio-temporal workload flexibility, few studies address the strategic sizing and allocation of diverse accelerator archetypes with different capital and energy-efficiency trade-offs. This paper unlocks the flexibility potential of heterogeneous accelerators by jointly sizing and allocating different AI workloads (training, delay-tolerant batch work, and online inference requests) across sites and hours to meet the time-varying grid constraint at minimum cost. Workload-specific throughput and energy coefficients link these decisions to facility power. In a representative two-site case, heterogeneous sizing reduces daily cost by 6.5% relative to spatio-temporal scheduling with fixed capacity. When evaluated using workload-specific performance, a capacity design based on workload-averaged hardware coefficients costs 7.7% more than the proposed design. The proposed design also eliminates the envelope violations of an unconstrained design at a 0.25\% daily cost increase. These results show how hardware heterogeneity and workload flexibility jointly shape economical, grid-compliant capacity plans.</summary>
    <category term="eess.SY" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
