    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27544v1</id>
    <title>Alliance Rupture and Repair in Simulating Cognitive Behavioral Therapy Dialogues</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T12:39:38Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27544v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27544v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>In cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT), routine work can strain the client-counselor working alliance, and the counselor must then notice the rupture, repair it, and adapt the work that follows. Existing CBT simulators model client resistance and follow structured treatment plans, but rarely track alliance state or allow counselors to repair ruptures over multiple turns and revise their plans accordingly. We present ALLY, a simulation framework that brings alliance rupture, repair, and replanning into ongoing CBT dialogue. Each client has a hidden alliance state that governs how openly the client engages. Like a real counselor, the counselor agent sees only the dialogue. It must notice when the client pulls back or pushes against the work, repair the rupture, and replan the rest of the session around what it has learned. Automatic evaluation shows that ALLY's counselors are closer to real CBT counselors than those of existing public synthetic datasets in strategy flow and emotional dynamics, and that ALLY is rated highest overall in counseling quality (CTRS) and interpersonal skill (FIS-T). An expert evaluation with 20 licensed therapists also finds the simulated ruptures plausible and the repairs and replanning largely appropriate.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
