    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27536v1</id>
    <title>Open CDLM: A Benchmark and Library for Continuous Diffusion Language Models</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T12:33:19Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27536v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27536v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Reliable comparisons of continuous diffusion language models (CDLMs) require evaluating both model fit and the distribution of generated text. Two common practices leave this goal only partially addressed. Likelihood-based evaluation often reports a variational upper bound on discrete negative log-likelihood, whose slack can confound comparisons. Generated-sample-based evaluation commonly combines generative perplexity with within-sequence unigram entropy; both scores can remain favorable even when a sampler produces the same text on every draw. We introduce \textbf{Open CDLM}, a benchmark for evaluating CDLMs and their samplers, and describe the design of an accompanying open-source library to support reproducible comparisons. For likelihood-based evaluation, we clarify which comparisons of model fit are supported by reported perplexity bounds and how those conclusions depend on the evaluation procedure. For sample-based evaluation, we use \textit{Kernel Text Discrepancy} (\textbf{KTD}) to compare collections of generated and real texts, alongside diagnostics for diversity, validity, and coverage. In controlled OpenWebText experiments, replacing a collection of texts with copies of a single selected passage improves both conventional generation scores. MAUVE detects the resulting collapse, and its response to other perturbations depends on the text representation and the type of perturbation. This work marks the beginning of Open CDLM as an open-source project for CDLM research. We will maintain and extend the library over time and welcome contributions from the community.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
