    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27500v1</id>
    <title>Turnout: Adaptive Attention Switching for Efficient Reasoning</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T11:49:13Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27500v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27500v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Post-hoc transformer linearization replaces the key-value cache of full attention, which grows linearly with generation length, with a fixed size state. This change reduces memory consumption but often hurts accuracy. In reasoning tasks, which require long generations, this effect is especially pronounced. In this work we introduce Turnout, a method that mitigates accuracy loss as a result of linearization, while maintaining its memory-reduction benefits. We apply post-hoc linearization to a pretrained full-attention model, training a linear path through distillation while leaving the original full-attention path unchanged. During response generation, the resulting hybrid model retains both paths and uses a learned policy to control when each layer switches from full to linear attention. We train the policy with reinforcement learning using a reward that balances answer correctness and memory use. Empirical evaluation of our method shows that the learned adaptive policy achieves a better accuracy-efficiency Pareto frontier on reasoning tasks compared to fixed switching and static hybrid baselines.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
