    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27463v1</id>
    <title>Supervaluationism about vagueness and indeterminacy: A judgment-aggregation perspective</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T11:12:40Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27463v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27463v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Supervaluationism about vagueness and indeterminacy is based on the idea that some sentences, or predicates within them, have multiple competing interpretations or specifications. A sentence is deemed determinately true if and only if it is true according to all specifications; and determinately false if and only if it is false according to all specifications. In all other cases, the sentence is assigned no determinate truth-value. Supervaluationism, however, is only one way of taking into account the verdicts of different specifications -- in technical terms, "aggregating" those verdicts. In this paper, we introduce insights from the theory of judgment aggregation to study a broad class of different aggregation functions. This allows us to offer an axiomatic characterization of supervaluationism, which, in turn, locates it precisely within a larger logical space of possible approaches to vagueness and indeterminacy. Among other results, we axiomatically compare supervaluationism with subvaluationism, the epistemic theory of vagueness, and Strong Kleene and LP logics.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-20T11:12:40Z</published>
