    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27445v1</id>
    <title>Structurally Different, Functionally Persistent: Capability Regions Under Model Optimization</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T10:41:20Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27445v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27445v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language model (LLM) capabilities have been associated with localized parameter regions, enabling targeted model editing and intervention. However, it remains unclear whether a region identified before model optimization remains an effective intervention target afterward. We evaluate the functional persistence and parameter overlap of coding capability regions across three instruction-tuned models under quantization, pruning, and LoRA-based adaptation. Although re-extracted regions exhibit substantial parameter reorganization, ablating the original region after optimization eliminates or nearly eliminates coding performance in almost all conditions, while magnitude-matched controls preserve performance. Partition analysis further shows that the shared subset of the original and re-extracted regions accounts for most of the observed ablation effect in the majority of conditions. These results demonstrate that parameter overlap and functional persistence are distinct: capability regions can remain causally effective intervention targets even when their parameter realization changes substantially.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-20T10:41:20Z</published>
