    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27444v1</id>
    <title>Silent Arbitration: How Large Language Models Resolve Co-Authoritative Conflicting Instructions</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T10:40:45Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27444v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27444v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language models increasingly receive high-level instructions about roles, values, interests, and communication style. Such instructions can conflict even when a prompt assigns them equal authority and provides no priority rule. We study co-authoritative persona conflict, in which a single prompt jointly assigns two opposing personas. Across eight conflict types grounded in published source material and five LLMs, we measure conflict acknowledgment, resolution strategy, and directional stance. Models acknowledge incompatibility in 72% of direct checks but explicitly surface it in only 22% of task responses, revealing silent arbitration. Dominance accounts for 45% of dual-persona responses, and 35% of compromise-labeled responses retain directional lean. Prompt framing further changes the visible form of arbitration substantially more than ordinary paraphrase variation. These findings show that evaluating instruction following should assess whether models disclose conflicts, how they resolve them, and which persona their responses favor.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-20T10:40:45Z</published>
